\title{Basis Criterion for Continuity}
\taxon{Proposition}
\import{macros}
\p{
    Let #{\mathcal{B}} be a [basis](def-005M) for the topology of #{Y}. A map
    #{f:X\to Y} is [continuous](def-005N) if and only if #{f^{-1}(B)} is open
    in #{X} for every #{B\in\mathcal{B}}.
}
\proof{
    The forward implication follows immediately from continuity because every
    basis element is open. Conversely, suppose the preimage of each basis
    element is open. Every open subset #{V\subseteq Y} is a union of basis
    elements, say #{V=\bigcup_{i\in I}B_i}. Preimages preserve arbitrary
    unions, so
    ##{
        f^{-1}(V)
        =f^{-1}\left(\bigcup_{i\in I}B_i\right)
        =\bigcup_{i\in I}f^{-1}(B_i).
    }
    The right-hand side is open in #{X}; hence #{f} is continuous.
}
